<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Leap Forward</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Leap Forward was a campaign launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> in 1958 to turn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> rapidly into an industrial and communist society. Peasants were organised into vast people's communes, labour was diverted to backyard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> furnaces, and officials reported inflated harvests while grain was requisitioned. The resulting famine of 1959–61 killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people, and the campaign was abandoned by 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward.jpg" alt="Great Leap Forward" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People%27s_commone_canteen3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The campaign grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s push for rapid development through mass mobilization and a reaction against the Soviet development strategy. Its core goal was to raise industrial and agricultural output by increasing labour inputs and so overcome <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s lack of material, technological and monetary resources. Before 1949 peasants had farmed small plots of their own, and it was recognized that a state monopoly on agriculture to finance industry would be unpopular, so peasants were to be brought under Party control in agricultural collectives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mao unveiled the campaign at a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in January 1958. An experimental commune at Chayashan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> in April 1958 abolished private plots and introduced communal kitchens, and in August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> decided that people's communes would be the new form of organization throughout rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. By the end of the year about 25,000 communes had been set up, averaging 5,000 households each, with work points replacing wages and money. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> production was to double within the year, mostly through backyard furnaces; the 1958 effort reached its target of 10.7 million tons, but more than 3 million tons of it was unusable.</p><p>On the communes, innovations based on the ideas of the Soviet agronomist Trofim Lysenko included close cropping and deep ploughing, which generally lowered grain production. Famine worsened when procurement quotas were set using false reports from the countryside. At the Lushan Conference of July and August 1959 the only senior leader to speak out openly was Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a>. Mao dismissed him and launched a campaign against rightist opportunism, in which over 3 million Party members were purged or penalized, and misreporting and coercive collection intensified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Much of the harvest was left to rot in some areas because labour had been diverted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and construction, and a locust swarm followed the killing of natural predators in the Four Pests Campaign. Not all deaths were from starvation: people were beaten or killed for reporting real harvest figures, refusing to hand over food or trying to flee. Armed rebellions occurred in several provinces, though they never seriously threatened the central government.</p><p>The Party studied the damage at conferences from 1960 to 1962, notably the Seven Thousand Cadres Conference in 1962, where Mao ceded day-to-day leadership to moderates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shaoqi">Liu Shaoqi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>. Mao acknowledged mistakes while holding that the three red flags could not be challenged, and in 1966 he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>. The campaign failed to industrialize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> rapidly, though it left substantial capital construction in iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, mining and textiles, and a gradual de-collectivization followed in the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Leap Forward” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1958 Mao launched the <b>Great Leap Forward</b>, merging collectives into vast people’s communes and demanding impossible targets for grain and steel; the resulting famine of 1959–61 killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people and was probably the deadliest in recorded history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Great Terror of 1937–1938, and millions passed through the camps of the Gulag; the famine caused by the <b>Great Leap Forward</b> in China killed tens of millions between 1959 and 1961; and the Khmer Rouge regime in Cambodia caused the deaths of between 1.5 and 2 million people…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Barbara Geddes found that single-party regimes last longer than military or personal dictatorships, and Steven Levitsky and Lucan Way have argued that where a revolutionary party built the army, as in China, the armed forces have not attempted coups even after disasters such as the <b>Great Leap Forward</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>In China the <b>Great Leap Forward</b> of 1957–1961 put peasants into large collective farms ordered to start heavy industry; agricultural output fell sharply, causing mass famine and millions of deaths.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1950s until the late 1970s Maoism was the political and military ideology of the Chinese Communist Party, and its mass campaigns, among them the Anti-Rightist Campaign of 1957–1958, the <b>Great Leap Forward</b> and the Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution, put Mao’s theory of cultural revolution into practice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The People’s Republic of China, founded in 1949, pursued land redistribution and the Anti-Rightist Movement, followed by the disastrous <b>Great Leap Forward</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
